{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## WITHDRAWING my correction — this ticket's ORIGINAL premise was right\n\nMy previous comment claimed `rebar transition` has no `--caused-by` flag and that every duplicate\ncame from blame auto-derivation. **That was wrong.** A peer session challenged it, I verified, and\nthe flag is live:\n\n- `_commands/transition.py:123-124,164` — `_parse_flags` parses and returns `--caused-by`.\n- `tests/interfaces/lifecycle/test_caused_by_autoderive.py:33` drives it through the CLI and passes.\n- Demonstrated in a fresh store with **zero commits and no `file_impact`**, where\n  `blame.derive_caused_by` could not have produced anything — the edge still named the flag's\n  culprit.\n\nMy experiment was invalid: I tested a **no-op** transition (`open open` on an already-open ticket),\nwhich returns early before any close-path flag handling. It never exercised a close.\n\n**So the original filing stands exactly as written**: passing `--caused-by` when an identical\n`caused_by` edge already exists creates a second duplicate edge. The `rebar link` → close sequence\nthe protocol encourages is the trigger, and the flag is a genuine participant, not a bystander.\n\nAlso withdrawn: my claim that AC3's different-origin path is \"unreachable from the CLI\". It is\nreachable — the flag reaches `_apply_caused_by` the same way the library seam does. No test\nre-anchoring is needed.\n\nThe one thing that survives from my correction: `rebar transition --help` genuinely omits\n`--caused-by`, because `_cli/help/transition.txt` and the help blob in `_commands/transition.py:65`\nare two sources that drifted when commit `0f999860ad` updated only the latter. That is a\ndiscoverability gap, now correctly scoped as `brokendown-moonlit-kiskadee`\n(`0276-7eea-baf0-48e3`, P3) — and it is why I misread the flag as absent in the first place.\n\nApologies for the churn on this ticket. The fix in change 1973 was never affected."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787270909371714001,"uuid":"a7669d82-d080-446b-a570-129b3fc9e734"}